{"groupId":"G2.6","requirement":"Recommendations are appropriate for the stated goals given access needs, term and foreseeable changes","reportSays":"The suitability report asserts three objectives: OBJ2 (long-term) and OBJ3 (short-term, x2). All five recommendations are linked to OBJ2 and/or OBJ3. The single new plan (NP1 - Aviva Pension Portfolio) is a personal pension with a selected retirement age of 75, accepting a transfer value of £110,185 with no regular contributions. The client prefers Flexi Access Drawdown when benefits are taken. Projections run to age 75 (low: £97,600, mid: £115,000, high: £135,000). The report notes state pension income will cover bills in retirement, allowing the pension to keep growing for large ad hoc spends. Retirement expenditure is expected to be approximately £1,700 per month. The cashflow runs to age 100 and no shortfall is projected. The report also notes the IHT treatment change from April 2027. No emergency fund or liquidity requirement is separately stated. No foreseeable life changes beyond retirement are noted in the recommendations section.","fileSays":"The fact find (B) records monthly household expenditure of £1,718 and a current monthly disposable income of -£288 (income £1,430 vs expenditure £1,718), indicating a current monthly shortfall. The policy research note (G) records a retirement income need of £1,700 net per month. The intelliflo cashflow baseline (F) shows employment income of £16,640 (client) and £13,200 (combined) with total regular expenditure of £24,001 in year one. The proposed scenario cashflow (F) confirms the plan runs to age 100, no shortfall projected, state pension covers bills in retirement, pension allowed to grow for large ad hoc spends. The Zurich policy info (E) confirms retirement date 7 June 2031 (age 75). The Aviva policy info (E) confirms drawdown is available from age 55 (57 from April 2028) and the plan must remain in pension until minimum pension age; income drawdown not possible after age 75 but funds can remain invested post-75 in drawdown. The People's Pension (E) confirms small pot access rules and FAD availability. The suitability report (I) links fund selection to 'retirement objectives in isolation as you requested' and states the cashflow uses a retirement age of 75. The critical yield table uses retirement age 75 throughout. No evidence of a separately documented emergency fund or liquidity reserve. No foreseeable life changes other than retirement are disclosed in any document.","discrepancies":["The report assigns OBJ3 as 'ShortTerm' but all five recommendations are a long-term pension consolidation; no recommendation explicitly addresses a short-term objective in a way that is distinguishable from the long-term pension goal, raising a question about what OBJ3 concretely is and how a pension consolidation serves it in the short term.","The current monthly cash flow shows a deficit of -£288/month (income £1,430 vs expenditure £1,718) yet the report does not address how near-term living costs are met or whether an emergency fund exists; no liquidity provision is identified in the recommendations.","The report states retirement expenditure of approximately £1,700 per month but the fact find records current monthly expenditure of £1,718 and current monthly income of £1,430, meaning the client is already spending more than they earn; the report does not reconcile this gap or address how the shortfall is currently funded.","The cashflow omits the People's Pension (£6,601.55, retained) from the listed inputs (only Aviva £3,744, Zurich £103,439, Standard Life £3,002 are listed), which may understate projected retirement assets, though the People's Pension is retained rather than transferred.","The mid growth rate is stated as 2.94% in the page 11 narrative of the suitability report but 2.95% in the critical yield table (page 18) and in the pension switch report; this is a minor internal inconsistency already noted in the extraction.","The report presents the recommendation as serving OBJ3 (short-term) without specifying what that short-term objective is or explaining how a long-term pension consolidation with a retirement age of 75 meets a short-term need."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The core appropriateness assessment can be performed. The recommended Aviva Pension Portfolio is a personal pension consolidating three existing pensions with a retirement age of 75, projections to age 75, and Flexi Access Drawdown as the intended decumulation method. The evidence (Zurich illustration, cashflow model, suitability report) consistently supports a retirement age of 75 and the FAD access mechanism. For a client aged 69 (born June 1956), the pension is already past the minimum pension age, so there is no access restriction that conflicts with the stated goal horizon — the guard regarding access mechanism applies here and no locked-capital concern arises. The state pension and cashflow evidence support the assertion that retirement income needs are broadly met, and the cashflow modelling (to age 100, no shortfall) corroborates the long-term planning horizon. However, two concerns remain after applying the guards. First, the linkage of all five recommendations to OBJ3 (short-term) is unexplained. The report does not define OBJ3 with sufficient specificity to assess whether a long-term pension consolidation genuinely serves a short-term objective, or whether this is boilerplate linkage. The guard requires a specific objective to be named; OBJ3's short-term classification against a pension consolidation is not evidenced. Second, the current monthly cash flow deficit (-£288/month) is not addressed anywhere in the recommendations. The fact find evidences that the client is currently spending £288/month more than their income, yet no emergency fund, liquidity buffer or near-term access plan is mentioned. The report's assertion that state pension will cover bills in retirement does not address the present shortfall. This is a material gap: access needs and foreseeable near-term financial pressure are not addressed. The People's Pension omission from the cashflow inputs is noted but does not materially change the no-shortfall conclusion given its small value (£6,601). The 2.94%/2.95% discrepancy is immaterial. No vulnerability characteristics are evidenced in the pack that would further affect this assessment. Overall, the recommendations are broadly appropriate for the long-term retirement objective, but the short-term objective linkage is unsupported and the current cash flow deficit and absence of any liquidity/emergency fund provision represent a material gap in the suitability analysis.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"G","category":"PolicyResearch","quote":"Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"B","category":"FactFind","quote":null,"cells":["Total Net Monthly Income","£ 1,430.00"]},{"passageId":"P24","source":"B","category":"FactFind","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Expenditure","£ 1,718.00"]},{"passageId":"P24","source":"B","category":"FactFind","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Disposable Income","£ -288.00"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I","category":"SuitabilityReport","quote":"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I","category":"SuitabilityReport","quote":"Your state pension income will cover your bills in retirement. Meaning your pension will be allowed to keep growing for large ad hoc spends.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I","category":"SuitabilityReport","quote":"The cashflow runs until you reach age 100 and assumes all surplus income throughout your lifetime is spent, rather than saved.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"I","category":"SuitabilityReport","quote":"This will  ensure you have a diversified investment portfolio based on your retirement objectives