{"groupId":"G3.5","requirement":"Investment strategy and asset allocation — the recommended funds, their risk ratings and allocations are consistent with the client's agreed risk profile and are supported by the evidence.","reportSays":"Target risk rating 5. Two funds recommended: HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc (50%, risk rating 5, OCF 0.18%) and CT Universal MAP Balanced C (50%, risk rating 5, OCF 0.29%). Strategy type: BespokePortfolio. Total allocation 100%. Asset allocation breakdown not extracted from the report.","fileSays":"The risk profile report (D) records a natural ATR of 4 (Cautious Balanced) from the psychometric questionnaire, but an agreed risk level of 5 (Balanced) following adviser discussion. The SIPP research report (G) confirms the agreed risk level is 5 and states 'The risk mapping of the new portfolio is 5 which matches the client's agreed risk level.' The research report lists the two funds at 50%/50%. The suitability report fund table shows both funds at risk rating 5, OCF 0.18% and 0.29% respectively. The HSBC fund factsheet (H) describes the fund's own internal risk profile as rated 3 on a 1–5 HSBC scale. The KIID for the HSBC fund (H) classifies it in SRRI category 4 (medium fluctuations). No KIID or factsheet in the pack assigns the HSBC fund a risk rating of 5 on any scale. The CT Universal MAP Balanced fund KIID (H) also shows SRRI category 4. The research report (G) shortlist confirms both funds are mapped to risk level 5 within the Defaqto/firm framework.","discrepancies":["The suitability report assigns both funds a risk rating of 5. The HSBC fund factsheet (H) states the fund's own risk profile is rated 3 on the HSBC 1–5 scale. The KIID (H) places it in SRRI category 4. Neither the factsheet nor the KIID assigns it a rating of 5 on any provider scale.","The CT Universal MAP Balanced KIID (H) shows SRRI category 4, not 5, at the fund level — yet the report assigns it a risk rating of 5.","The questionnaire-derived natural ATR is 4 (Cautious Balanced) but the agreed/override rating used for the strategy is 5 (Balanced). The report records an upward override.","Asset allocation breakdown is absent from the extracted model; the report does not state the underlying asset class split explicitly, so alignment of asset allocation with risk level 5 cannot be directly verified."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Explicit asset allocation breakdown from the suitability report (path /solution/investmentStrategy/assetAllocation[] absent from canonical model)"],"analysis":"The core comparison — whether the strategy risk rating (5) matches the client's agreed risk rating (5) — is fully supported. The risk profile report [P22] records the agreed level as 5 Balanced, and the SIPP research report [P16] explicitly confirms 'The risk mapping of the new portfolio is 5 which matches the client's agreed risk level.' The 50/50 fund split totals 100% and is corroborated by both [P8] and [P11].\n\nThe discrepancies between the fund-level risk ratings stated in the report (both 5) and the provider's own factsheet/KIID ratings (HSBC: HSBC-scale 3; SRRI 4; CT: SRRI 4) are explained by the guard: fund-level ratings from a provider are not necessarily on the firm's client risk scale. The Defaqto research framework maps these funds to risk level 5 within the firm's own scale, which is the scale used throughout the advice process. The research report [P14] shortlist shows both funds appearing in the risk level 5 cohort, and [P16] confirms the portfolio mapping. This is sufficient to explain the apparent mismatch between fund-factsheet ratings and the firm's rating of 5.\n\nThe upward override from ATR 4 to agreed level 5 is recorded in [P22] and is explained there: the adviser discussed projections with the client and adjusted the risk level through an interactive process, resulting in an agreed level of 5. The report does not appear to assert that the questionnaire produced a score of 5 — the ambiguity is noted in the extraction but the override is documented in the risk profile report with a client-agreed outcome. This is a potential concern in its own right (upward override) but falls within CHK-003's scope; however, the override is documented and the client agreed it, mitigating the concern to moderate rather than high severity.\n\nThe absence of an explicit asset allocation breakdown in the extracted model means direct verification of asset class splits against a risk-level-5 model cannot be performed. The KIID for the HSBC fund [P2] states the asset allocation under typical conditions as Equities 45–70%, Bonds 20–55%, Other 0–25%, which is consistent with a balanced/medium risk profile. No contradicting asset allocation is in evidence.\n\nOverall, the material elements of the strategy are supported. The chief residual concern is the upward override from questionnaire score 4 to agreed level 5: while documented, it is an upward adjustment and warrants flagging at Moderate severity.","citations":[{"passageId":"P22","source":"D/Risk profile report.docx","category":"D","quote":"Your natural risk level is based on the answers given in the Attitude to Risk Questionnaire which was completed on 18/09/2025.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P22","source":"D/Risk profile report.docx","category":"D","quote":null,"cells":["4  Cautious Balanced","As a Cautious Balanced investor you tend to see yourself as quite a cautious person and are inclined to view risk negatively rather than as a source of opportunity."]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"Following an interactive discussion, and considering your natural attitude to risk, capacity for loss and required return for your goal, we agreed a risk level.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["5  Balanced","As a Balanced investor you do not see yourself as a particularly cautious person and have no strong positive or negative associations with the notion of taking risk."]},{"passageId":"P16","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"The client's agreed risk level is 5. The risk mapping of the new portfolio is 5 which matches the client's agreed risk level.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"I/Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc","50%","5","0.18%"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"I/Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced C","50%","5","0.29%"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc (GBP)","Columbia Threadneedle Fund Management Limited","50.00"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio Accumulation C (GBP)","HSBC Asset Management (Fund Services UK) Limited","50.00"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"H/Fund Factsheet (1).pdf","category":"H","quote":"The Fund's risk profile is rated as 3 where 1 is a lower level of risk and 5 is a higher level of risk.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"H/KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The Fund is rated 4 because historically it has shown a medium level of volatility (how much the value of the Fund goes up and down).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"H/KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"This Fund is classified in category 4 because its price or simulated data has shown medium fluctuations historically.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"G/SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"6 investments from a total of 6 risk level  investments, as rated by Defaqto, matched the filters applied.","cells":null}],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

