e bullets are repeated verbatim across the Aviva, Zurich and Standard Life recommendations: 'The new scheme offers access to a wider range of investment options, allowing a more tailored investment strategy that better suits the client's risk profile and retirement goals'; 'The new scheme allows adviser charges to be deducted directly, ensuring ongoing financial planning support without separate payments'; 'The adviser will have the ability to actively monitor and adjust investments as required.' These are boilerplate shared across recommendations rather than plan-specific rationale.","The policy research file (P8) records the objective field for each plan recommendation as blank (no objective listed), and the rationale notes are brief ('Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing') compared to the fuller narrative in the suitability report. The research document does not corroborate the specific linked objectives OBJ2/OBJ3 by name.","No retirement income projection is present in the report or the file (flagged as NotPresentInDocument in extraction), leaving the assertion that the recommendations position the client 'well-positioned for retirement' unsupported by projected outcomes.","The Scottish Widows recommendation presents two alternative outcomes (encash now for ~£170 net vs retain to retirement for potential full tax-free receipt) without unambiguously stating which is the single recommended course of action, though the extraction notes the £170 net receipt appears to be the recommended course.","The risk profile report (P11) records 'No additional notes were recorded' for both the investment target discussion and the agreed risk level discussion, meaning the override of ATR from 4 to 5 and its link to the recommended solution is not documented in the risk profile evidence beyond the ambiguity noted in the extraction."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report provides rationale for all five recommendations and links them to objectives, satisfying the basic requirement. Several elements are genuinely client-specific: the People's Pension retention rationale (active employer contributions) is plan-specific and corroborated by P8; the Scottish Widows rationale (no suitable single-fund at risk 5, no adviser charging) is corroborated by P1 which confirms adviser charging is not available; the Zurich rationale (no risk-5 funds, blended funds underperformed) is specific to that provider. However, three material concerns arise. First, the most serious: the Standard Life cost rationale states a 0.08% saving, but the charges table shows +0.26% increase, and the derived figures demonstrate the EA5 row is computed on the wrong fund value entirely. This is a direct internal contradiction within the report itself on a factual matter central to the switch justification — the rationale claims cost saving where the report's own charges table and arithmetic show a cost increase. This is not explained by any guard and constitutes a genuine finding. Second, three rationale bullets are repeated verbatim across the Aviva, Zurich and Standard Life recommendations. Under the guard, identical verbatim repetition is boilerplate. While some generic benefits (adviser charging access, investment range) legitimately apply to all three switches, their word-for-word duplication without plan-specific adaptation reduces the client-specificity of the rationale for the Aviva and Standard Life recommendations in particular. Third, the absence of a retirement income projection (flagged as NotPresentInDocument) means the assertion that the client will be 'well-positioned for retirement' is unsupported by projected outcomes. The Scottish Widows dual-outcome presentation is a lower-level concern as the recommended course appears identifiable. The vulnerability overlay: no specific vulnerability drivers are identified for C1 in the evidence (C2 fields are absent/not applicable as this appears a sole client case), so no material impact on this check. The Standard Life cost contradiction is the dominant finding — a rationale asserting cost saving where the report's own table and derived arithmetic show a cost increase is a High-severity issue under the severity guidance ('rationale that contradicts the case facts').","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva","Recommendation","Switch"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva","Why/Why isnt this scheme suitable for the client?","Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich","Why/Why isnt this scheme suitable for the client?","Expensive, no adviser servicing"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["People's Pension","Recommendation","Retain"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["People's Pension","Why/Why isnt this scheme suitable for the client?","Active workplace pension"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Can adviser charging be applied","No","","1","4","No","","1","21","Yes","","1","2","No","","1","4","No","","1","1"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"Investment: £110,185 then £0 monthly","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"most consistent risk adjusted returns, over the preceding 5 years discrete period, within the Defaqto MPS Comparator peer group.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Risk profile report.docx","category":"D","quote":"Your natural risk level is based on the answers given in the Attitude to Risk Questionnaire which was completed on 18/09/2025.","cells"