:"The Aviva Key Features document (P4, P7, P14) discloses: capital/pension pot value can go down as well as up; currency risk (sterling strengthening reduces value); property liquidity delay risk; fixed-term product access restriction; cancellation period risk; income drawdown specific risks (impact on means-tested benefits; fund depletion if income too high; less for dependants or annuity; tax position uncertainty; risk of overpaying tax). The Aviva Terms and Conditions (P10, P13) disclose: dilution levy risk; SDRT on surrender of units; fund manager charges including those taken from capital rather than income. The HSBC KIID (P1, P3, P6, P8, P9) discloses: counterparty risk; credit risk; default risk; derivatives risk; currency risk; liquidity risk; risk and reward category 4 (medium fluctuations); minimum recommended holding period of 5 years; income reinvested. The Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11, P12) discloses: derivative risk (losses can exceed initial investment); fixed interest securities risk (interest rate changes); credit risk; smaller companies risk; emerging markets risk; liquidity risk; currency risk; leverage risk. The Zurich Policy Info (P16, P17) discloses: property fund valuation and delay risk (up to 12 months); high yield bond fund risk; smaller companies/concentrated portfolio/less mature market risk; political/economic risk in less developed markets; fund managers taking yearly management charge from capital; derivative counterparty risk; OEIC cross-liability risk; fixed-interest inflation/interest rate risk. The SIPP research report (P15) records the agreed risk level as 5 and lists CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund and HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio as the recommended funds, with MiFID II pre-sale cost disclosures including transaction costs noted.","discrepancies":["The report does not mention counterparty risk, which is identified in both the HSBC KIID (P3) and the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11) as a material risk not fully captured by the risk and reward indicator.","The report does not mention credit risk or default risk on bonds, disclosed in the HSBC KIID (P3) and Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11) as material risks for the recommended funds.","The report does not mention derivatives risk (including that derivatives losses can exceed the initial investment), disclosed in the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11) and HSBC KIID (P3).","The report does not mention currency risk, disclosed in both KIIDs (P3, P11) and the Aviva Key Features (P4) as applicable to the recommended funds.","The report does not mention leverage risk, disclosed in the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11).","The report does not mention emerging markets risk, disclosed in the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11).","The report does not mention smaller companies risk, disclosed in the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11).","The report does not mention interest rate / fixed interest securities risk, disclosed in the Columbia Threadneedle KIID (P11) and Zurich Policy Info (P17).","The report does not mention the dilution levy / SDRT risk disclosed in the Aviva Terms and Conditions (P10).","The report does not mention income drawdown-specific risks (impact on means-tested benefits; risk of fund depletion from high income; reduced funds for dependants/annuity; tax position uncertainty) disclosed in the Aviva Key Features (P4), though drawdown is a stated benefit option.","The report does not mention the minimum recommended holding period of 5 years for the HSBC fund (P9), relevant given the client's pension access timeline.","The report's transaction cost exclusion caveat (p.10) is present, but the MiFID II pre-sale cost disclosures in the SIPP research report (P15) include transaction costs; there is no reconciliation in the report of how these were communicated to the client.","The report acknowledges the recommended Aviva funds have not outperformed the Zurich funds (p.13) but does not address whether this underperformance, combined with the higher charges on the Standard Life plan (the charges table shows +0.26% impact, contradicting the page 8 narrative of a 0.08% saving), constitutes a material disadvantage that has been fully explained."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report contains a reasonable base layer of risk disclosure: capital-at-risk wording, market risk, liquidity (access timing), minimum access age, IHT legislative change, cancellation risks, relative performance acknowledgement, projection assumption caveats, and transaction cost exclusion notice. These satisfy the general capital-at-risk warning but the guard requires product-specific risks to be tested separately. When tested against the KIIDs and product literature on file, several material product-specific risks present in those documents are absent from the report: counterparty risk, credit/default risk, derivatives risk (including losses exceeding initial investment from the CT fund KIID), currency risk, leverage risk, emerging markets risk, smaller companies risk, and interest rate / fixed interest securities risk. These are not generic boilerplate omissions — they are explicitly identified as material risks in the KIIDs for the two specific recommended funds (CT Universal MAP Balanced and HSBC Global Strategy Balanced). The guard against deferring risk disclosure to product literature applies here: the report does not cross-reference these KIIDs or state that the client has been directed to read them for these specific risks, nor does it reproduce them. The income drawdown risks in the Aviva Key Features (fund depletion from high income withdrawals; impact on means-tested benefits) are also absent, which is material given the product's flexibility to take drawdown. The minimum 5-year recommended holding period for the HSBC fund (P9) is not mentioned, which has relevance to the client's access timeline. The Standard Life charge discrepancy (report page 8 says 0.08% saving; table page 11 shows +0.