 cash do not apply to this plan.' Drawdown options are not available on the Zurich plan but it can be transferred. [P9] Standard Life policy info confirms transfer value available; no specific guarantees flagged in retrieved text. [P14/P10] Scottish Widows confirms: 'Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission'; 'no guarantees, discounts or bonuses attached to this member's plan'; employer and employee contributions are ongoing. [P16] File note confirms: Scottish Widows discounted as 'no servicing and reduced flexibility'; Aviva alternative discounted because only mixed asset fund is risk rated 4 (does not meet ATR); Zurich internal switch discounted because no risk-5 multi-asset funds, blend used has not performed as well; Standard Life discounted as all funds in 20-60% equities range are more expensive than Aviva offer. [P5/P6] Pension switching report (Defaqto) shows new solution ranked 5th by maturity value (£125,000) against existing solution ranked 2nd (£127,000); Zurich standalone ranked 1st (£128,000); the new solution produces a lower projected maturity value than simply staying with the existing pensions. [P20] Pension switch report summary confirms comparison was conducted and recommendation is to consolidate into Aviva Platform Pension Portfolio Choice SIPP. [P21] Suitability report comparison table shows Scottish Widows does not allow servicing adviser; Standard Life does allow servicing adviser. [P22] Charges comparison table confirms Aviva existing 0.50%/£18.72, impact -0.06%; but for Standard Life the impact column shows +0.26% whereas the page 8 narrative states the plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' — these are contradictory. The derived figures confirm EA5 charge rows are computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) not Standard Life's (£3,002), making the Standard Life cost comparison unreliable.","discrepancies":["The report states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (page 8 narrative, a cost saving in favour of switching). The charges comparison table (page 11) shows the impact of the recommended alternative for Standard Life as +0.26% (an increase — i.e. the new plan is MORE expensive for Standard Life, not cheaper). These are directly contradictory.","The derived figures confirm that the Standard Life (EA5) charge rows in the comparison table are computed using Zurich's current fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own value (£3,002), rendering the entire Standard Life cost comparison arithmetically unreliable.","The Pension switching report (Defaqto, P5/P6) ranks the new solution 5th by projected maturity value (£125,000) against the existing combined solution ranked 2nd (£127,000). The report asserts 'lower costs' and net benefit from switching but the independent comparison tool shows the new solution produces a lower projected maturity value than retaining the existing pensions — a material contradiction of the cost/benefit rationale.","The report claims switching Aviva gives 'lower costs' and the existing Aviva plan is '0.06% more expensive'. The Defaqto report ranks Aviva standalone 3rd (£126,000 projected), also higher than the new solution (£125,000), which is consistent with the new solution being slightly cheaper on charges but also slightly worse in projected outcome — the net-benefit narrative of 'lower costs therefore better outcome' is weakened by the projection evidence.","The report's net-benefit conclusion for the Zurich switch does not claim a cost saving but the charges table shows a -0.19% impact (a saving), which is directionally consistent; however no discrepancy here — the report simply did not claim a cost saving for Zurich.","The report does not address the fact that the new solution has a lower projected maturity value (£125,000) than the existing solution (£127,000) per the Defaqto switching report, nor does it explain why this is outweighed by other benefits for the client.","The Scottish Widows confirmation (P14/P10) states the plan 'does not support ongoing advisor charging' — this corroborates the report's no-adviser-management rationale. No discrepancy on this point.","The Aviva policy info (P8) confirms the Lifestage Investment Approach is active — this corroborates the report's lifestyling rationale. No discrepancy on this point.","The Zurich policy info (P3) confirms no guarantees, no GMP, no protected tax-free cash — this corroborates the report's implicit assertion that no safeguarded benefits are lost on the Zurich transfer. No discrepancy on this point.","The report states the new plan is overall '0.24% less expensive' on a like-for-like basis but '4.24% more expensive' including adviser fees (per internal inconsistency noted in extraction). The report presents both figures but does not clearly reconcile them for the client; the monetary difference is £264 vs £4,672 — a material gap. This is partially addressed in the charges section but the net-benefit conclusions do not acknowledge that including adviser fees the arrangement is materially more expensive."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The replacement rationale rests on several planks: (1) cost savings, (2) greater investment flexibility and adviser management capability, (3) removal of lifestyling risk, (4) consolidation. The file evidence is mixed in its support.\n\nSupporting the rationale: The Aviva policy info [P8] confirms lifestyling is active, directly corroborating the lifestyling risk argument. The Scottish Widows confirmations [P10, P14] confirm no adviser charging is supported, corroborating that rationale. The Zurich policy info [P3] confirms no safeguarded benefits, GMP or protected tax-free cash, so no safeguarded benefits analysis was needed and none being present is not itself a gap. The file notes [P16] record that alternatives (internal fund switches, retaining at existing providers) were considered and discounted with reasons. The Defaqto comparison [P5, P20] provides documented alternative comparison including a stakeholder pension option.\n\nContradicting the rationale: The most serious discrepancy is the Standard Life cost comparison. The report's narrative (page 8) states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' — i.e. switching saves money. The charges comparison table shows +0.26% (new plan is more expensive). The derived figures confirm this table row was computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's (£3,002), making the entire Standard Life cost comparison wrong regardless of which direction the error runs. The cost-saving rationale for the Standard Life switch is therefore unsupported and potentially inverted.\n\nA further material concern is that the Defaqto switching report [P6] shows the new solution (£125,000 projected at mid-growth) underperforms the existing combined solution (£127,000) and also underperforms a standalone Zurich retention (£128,000) and Aviva retention (£126,000). The report asserts net benefit from lower costs and better outcomes, but the switching report's own projections show the new solution produces a lower projected maturity value. The report does not explain this discrepancy or argue why non-cost benefits outweigh the projected value shortfall.\n\nThe '4.24% more expensive including adviser fees' figure versus '0.24% cheaper on a like-for-like basis' is disclosed in the charges section but is not reconciled in the net-benefit conclusions, which state 'lower costs' without the qualifier that this is before adviser charges.\n\nThe Standard Life cost comparison error is the highest-severity issue: the reported rationale states a cost saving that the report's own charges table contradicts, and the derived figures confirm the table was computed on the wrong fund value. This is a direct internal contradiction of a replacement rationale claim. The projected maturity value shortfall versus the existing solution, unaddressed in the report, is a further material omission.\n\nNo vulnerability flags are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this assessment — the file notes [P18] record capacity for loss and ATR considerations but no vulnerability characteristics that would heighten the concern about the replacement rationale specifically.\n\nThe guard regarding charges rationale being contradicted by a charges schedule showing the opposite applies here and confirms this is a genuine finding, not a spurious mismatch.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Does the plan have an auto-switching investment approach? Yes, Lifestage Investment Approach","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SW Further Info 4.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Scottish Widows Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"File notes notes - updated.md","category":"C","quote":"Standard Life – Alternative option\nDiscounted as all funds within the same range of 20-60% equities are more expensive than what we can offer at Aviva.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"File notes notes - updated.md","category":"C","quote":"Zurich – Alternative option\nZurich do not have risk level 5 funds, a blend of a 7 and a 2 has been used to create the below 5. They have no multi asset funds in his risk range either","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["1","Product: Zurich - Zurich pension Platform: None","0.2%","£128,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["2","Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["5","New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Projected value at age 75 (Mid growth)","£127,000","£119,000","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","+£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva","0.50%","£18.72","-0.06%","-£2.24"]}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

