{"groupId":"G9.5","requirement":"Switching costs exceed the benefits — the suitability report must demonstrate that the costs of switching do not exceed the benefits, with evidence supporting the cost/benefit comparison for each ceding plan.","reportSays":"The report asserts: (1) No exit penalties on any of the three switched plans (Aviva, Zurich, Standard Life). (2) Aviva existing plan is 0.50% p.a. with annual charge £18.72; switching saves -0.06% / -£2.25 p.a. (3) Zurich existing plan is 0.93% p.a. with annual charge £961.98; switching saves -0.49% / -£506.85 p.a. (4) Standard Life existing plan is 0.18% p.a. with annual charge £186.19; the recommended plan is 0.26% MORE expensive / +£268.94 p.a. (5) Page 8 narrative states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (implying a saving), contradicting the +0.26% shown in the charges table on page 11. (6) On a like-for-like basis the Aviva plan is 0.24% less expensive than current plans; including adviser fees it is 4.24% more expensive, requiring £4,672 outperformance. (7) Critical yields are stated as 1.9% (low), 4.8% (mid), 7.8% (high) — described as the growth rate required from the new solution to match projected maturity value. (8) Out-of-market risk during transition is disclosed. (9) Scottish Widows encashment under small pots rules estimated to return £170 net. (10) Benefits cited include lower costs (Aviva and Zurich), lifestyling correction, greater investment choice, adviser charging facilitation, and ongoing monitoring.","fileSays":"The pension switch report (Defaqto/switch report document) confirms: (1) Existing pension fund value at switch: £110,185.60; new solution day-one value: £106,880.60, a shortfall of £3,305.00. (2) Critical yields for the overall comparison (Zurich-only section and combined) are 1.9% (low, 0.3% growth), 4.8% (mid, 2.95% growth), 7.8% (high, 5.88% growth) — matching the report's stated figures. (3) Effect of switching at mid growth: -£12,000 / -9.4%; at low: -£10,400 / -9.6%; at high: -£15,000 / -10.0%. (4) Standard Life only comparison: existing RIY 0.6%, new solution RIY 0.6%; effect of switching at mid growth only -£10 / -0.3%; critical yield 6.0% (mid). (5) Aviva only comparison: RIY existing 0.6%, new solution 0.6%; effect of switching at mid -£30 / -0.6%; critical yield 5.9% (high). (6) The switch report does not contain the per-plan annual charge figures (£18.72, £961.98, £186.19) or the percentage differentials (-0.06%, -0.49%, +0.26%) cited from page 11 of the suitability report. These figures are not corroborated by the retrieved evidence. (7) The switch report shows the Zurich plan has a much higher RIY in the new solution (2.1–2.3%) versus existing (0.3%), reflecting materially higher costs in the new plan for the Zurich money specifically. (8) No exit penalties are referenced in the switch report. (9) Features/guarantees lost: not addressed in either the suitability report or the switch report.","discrepancies":["1. Standard Life charge comparison contradiction: the suitability report's page 8 narrative states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (a saving), while the charges table on page 11 states the recommended plan is +0.26% MORE expensive than Standard Life — a direct internal contradiction that is confirmed by derived figures showing the +0.26% row is computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) not Standard Life's (£3,002), making the charge impact figure unreliable.","2. EA5 (Standard Life) charge row computed on wrong fund value: derived figures confirm the £186.19 / 0.18% row and the +£268.94 / +0.26% impact row are arithmetically consistent with Zurich's fund value (~£103,439), not Standard Life's £3,002 — meaning the cost comparison for Standard Life is calculated on the wrong plan value.","3. Per-plan annual charge figures (£18.72, £961.98, £186.19) and differentials (-0.06%, -0.49%, +0.26%) from the suitability report page 11 charges table are not corroborated by any retrieved passage — the switch report does not contain these figures.","4. The switch report shows the Zurich plan's new-solution RIY is 2.1–2.3% versus existing 0.3%, indicating substantially higher costs in the new arrangement for Zurich money, while the suitability report claims a 0.49% per annum saving — these are directionally inconsistent (the switch report shows a net cost increase, not a saving, when expressed as RIY).","5. Immediate fund value reduction at switch: £110,185.60 existing versus £106,880.60 day-one new solution — a £3,305 shortfall — is evidenced in the switch report but does not appear to be disclosed in the suitability report's switching costs section.","6. Critical yield at mid growth is 4.8%, which exceeds the stated mid growth rate of 2.95% (or 2.94% per page 11), meaning the new solution is projected to underperform the existing at mid growth — this is evidenced in the switch report but the suitability report does not appear to address that the critical yield exceeds the mid growth assumption.","7. Features or guarantees lost: the extraction confirms this field is absent from the report for all five existing arrangements (EA1–EA5) — there is no analysis of whether any safeguarded benefits, guarantees or valuable features are lost on transfer from any ceding plan.","8. Page 11 narrative states 'like-for-like 0.24% less expensive' but 'including adviser fees 4.24% more expensive / £4,672 outperformance required' — the two figures measure different things and are both present but the switch report does not corroborate the like-for-like 0.24% figure.","9. The suitability report states critical yield of 1.9% (growth rate required from new solution to match projected maturity value) but the switch report labels the 1.9% critical yield in the context of the overall combined comparison, while the Zurich-only section shows a 0.3% critical yield at low growth — the basis and scope of the cited critical yields need to be read carefully.","10. Scottish Widows encashment (£211.32, EA2): the report states £170 net under small pots rules but no switch report analysis or provider documentation is available to corroborate the tax calculation or encashment terms."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Provider documentation confirming absence of safeguarded benefits or guarantees for each of the five ceding plans","Independent corroboration of per-plan annual charge figures (£18.72, £961.98, £186.19) and differentials from the suitability report page 11 — not present in the switch report","Corroboration of the like-for-like 0.24% cost saving figure","Scottish Widows encashment documentation / small pots tax analysis"],"analysis":"The check reveals several material concerns. First and most serious: the Standard Life charge comparison (EA5) is computed on the wrong fund value. Derived figures confirm that both the existing annual charge row (£186.19 at 0.18%) and the impact row (+£268.94 at 0.26%) are arithmetically consistent with Zurich's fund value (~£103,439), not Standard Life's own £3,002. This means the cost comparison for Standard Life is fundamentally unreliable. Separately, the suitability report contains a direct internal contradiction between page 8 (Standard Life is 0.08% more expensive, implying a saving) and the page 11 table (+0.26%, a cost increase) — a finding already flagged in the extraction's internalInconsistencies. The switch report provides RIY data for the Zurich plan showing the new solution RIY is 2.1–2.3% versus the existing 0.3% — a material cost increase in the new arrangement for Zurich's money — while the suitability report claims a 0.49% annual saving. These are directionally inconsistent. The non-financial benefits cited (lifestyling correction, risk alignment, multi-asset fund at correct risk level) could in principle justify a net cost increase for Zurich under the guard, but the report does not acknowledge the RIY increase or the fact that the critical yield (4.8% at mid growth) exceeds the mid growth assumption (2.95%), meaning underperformance against the existing arrangement is the projected outcome at mid growth. The £3,305 immediate shortfall at the point of switch is evidenced in the switch report but not disclosed in the suitability report's switching costs section. No analysis of features or guarantees lost is present for any of the five ceding plans — this is confirmed as absent by the extraction (NotPresentInDocument for EA1–EA5 featuresOrGuaranteesLost), representing a highest-severity gap per the severity guidance. The per-plan charge figures in the suitability report's page 11 table are not corroborated by any retrieved passage. Taken together: the Standard Life cost comparison is computed on the wrong plan's fund value, the critical yield exceeds the mid growth rate without acknowledgement, the immediate fund value shortfall is undisclosed, and features/guarantees lost are unaddressed for all plans. These are not explained by any false-positive guard. The non-financial benefits guard could partially explain the cost increase for Zurich but does not cure the absence of safeguarded benefits analysis or the wrong-fund-value error for Standard Life. The vulnerability overlay: no specific vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this analysis beyond what is already recorded.","citations":[{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Low growth rate – (0.00%)","Existing pension: £108,000","New solution: £97,600","Reduction in yield: 0.3% / 2.1%","Critical yield: 1.9%","Effect of switching: £10,400 or 9.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension: £127,000","New solution: £115,000","Reduction in yield: 0.3% / 2.2%","Critical yield: 4.8%","Effect of switching: £12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["High growth rate – (5.88%)","Existing pension: £150,000","New solution: £135,000","Reduction in yield: 0.3% / 2.3%","Critical yield: 7.8%","Effect of switching: £15,000 or 10.0% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension: £110,185.60","New solution day one: £106,880.60","Shortfall: £3,305.00"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - SL pension only","Low growth: existing £2,910 / new £2,900","Reduction in yield: 0.6% / 0.6%","Critical yield (mid): 6.0%"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Reduction in yield: 0.6% / 0.6%","Critical yield: 6.0%","Effect of switching: £30 or 0.6% decrease"]}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

