{"groupId":"G9.2","requirement":"Benefits and costs of switching must be disclosed, compared per ceding plan, and where costs are higher the report must demonstrate the benefits outweigh them — all consistent with and supported by the evidence.","reportSays":"The report asserts: (1) No exit penalties on any ceding plan. (2) Aviva (EA3): existing plan 0.50% / £18.72 pa; switch saves 0.06% / £2.25 pa. (3) Zurich (EA4): existing plan 0.93% / £961.98 pa; switch saves 0.49% / £506.85 pa. (4) Standard Life (EA5): existing plan 0.18% / £186.19 pa; switch costs an additional 0.26% / £268.94 pa — yet the recommendation rationale on p8 states the existing plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. a saving). (5) Like-for-like the new plan is 0.24% cheaper / £264 pa underperformance tolerance; including adviser fee the new plan requires £4,672 outperformance pa (4.24% more expensive). (6) Critical yield at mid-growth rate (described as 2.94% in p11 narrative, 2.95% in the switch report table) is 4.8% to age 75. (7) Benefits claimed: lower costs, elimination of lifestyling risk, greater investment choice, adviser-charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring, multi-asset fund alignment. (8) Scottish Widows (EA2): encashed under small pots rules, estimated net receipt £170 (25% tax-free, 75% at marginal rate on £200 fund). (9) No safeguarded benefits or features lost are mentioned anywhere in the report for any ceding plan.","fileSays":"[P6] (Pension switch report) shows the combined three-plan comparison: existing pension projected £127,000 at mid-growth (2.95%), new solution £125,000; RIY existing 0.3%, new solution 0.6%; critical yield 3.2%; effect of switching £2,000 / 1.6% decrease at mid. [P3] shows Zurich-only comparison: existing £120,000 mid, new solution £118,000 mid; RIY existing 0.3%, new 0.6%; critical yield 3.6%; effect -£2,000 / 1.6%. [P2] shows Standard Life-only comparison: new solution £3,430 mid vs existing £3,440; effect -£10 / 0.3% decrease; critical yield 6.0% (very high relative to fund size). [P12] shows the full comparison including adviser charges: new solution projects £115,000 vs existing £127,000 at mid-growth; RIY new 2.2%; critical yield 4.8%; effect -£12,000 / 9.4% at mid. [P7] confirms existing pension total £110,185.60, new solution day-one value £106,880.60, death-benefit shortfall £3,305 immediately post-switch. [P9] (Zurich policy info) confirms: transfer value/early retirement charge — no deductions taken from current value; fund switch charge £0.00. [P13] (policy research) confirms no MVR on any plan. [P14] (Scottish Widows) confirms no protected tax-free cash, lifestyle arrangement may be affected by retirement age changes. [P16] (Standard Life) shows employer contribution payments history, meaning Standard Life was a former workplace scheme with employer contributions. [P11] (KIID) confirms 0.18% ongoing charge for one of the recommended funds. The switch report at [P6] uses 2.95% as the mid-growth rate, while the suitability report narrative at [P1] states 2.94%. The switch report at [P2] shows Standard Life new-solution critical yield of 6.0% at low growth, and the effect of switching Standard Life is only -£10 at mid-growth, a trivially small difference — yet the charges table in the report shows Standard Life switching adds 0.26% pa cost. No passage in the file addresses whether any safeguarded benefits, guaranteed annuity rates, or other protected features exist for any of the five plans.","discrepancies":["Standard Life (EA5) cost direction contradiction: the report's recommendation rationale (p8) states the existing Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. a saving), but the charges comparison table (p11) shows the impact as +0.26% (the recommended plan is MORE expensive). These directly contradict each other.","EA5 charge arithmetic computed on wrong fund value: derived figures show the EA5 charges row (£186.19 at 0.18% and £268.94 at 0.26%) imply a fund value of ~£103,439, which is Zurich's value, not Standard Life's value of £3,002. The EA5 rows in the charges table are therefore computed on the wrong plan's fund value, making the Standard Life cost comparison unreliable.","Mid-growth rate inconsistency: the suitability report narrative (p11/p1) states 2.94%, but the pension switch report table [P6] uses 2.95%. These are not identical.","Critical yield figure: the suitability report states critical yield at mid-growth is 4.8% (including adviser charges). [P12] also shows 4.8% at mid-growth including charges, which is consistent. However [P6] (charges-only basis) shows 3.2% — the report does not clearly distinguish between these two figures when citing 4.8%.","Standard Life-only critical yield at low growth is 6.0% per [P2], a very high hurdle for a small pot, but the report's narrative does not address this per-plan critical yield for Standard Life specifically.","No safeguarded benefits analysis: the report contains no mention of features or guarantees that may be lost on transfer for any of the five ceding plans (extraction confirms these paths are NotPresentInDocument). No provider documentation is cited confirming absence of such features for Zurich, Aviva, Standard Life or People's Pension transfers.","Standard Life was a former workplace scheme (employer contributions confirmed in [P16]). The report does not address whether any employer contributions would be lost or whether the scheme is still active.","Death benefit shortfall of £3,305 immediately post-switch ([P7]) is evidenced in the switch report but the suitability report does not appear to address this immediate reduction in death benefit coverage.","The report states a like-for-like saving of 0.24%/£264 pa and separately a requirement to outperform by 4.24%/£4,672 pa including adviser fee. These are both present but not reconciled for the client in a way that clearly shows the net cost position per plan.","Scottish Widows £170 net receipt and 25%/75% tax split: no supporting evidence in the file corroborates the specific £170 figure, the £200 fund value, or the tax calculation — these figures are unmatched in the retrieved passages."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Several material concerns arise. First and most serious: the Standard Life (EA5) cost comparison is internally contradictory and arithmetically unreliable. The recommendation narrative says switching saves 0.08% on Standard Life, but the charges table shows switching adds 0.26% — a direct contradiction flagged in the extraction's own internal inconsistencies. Compounding this, the derived figures establish that the EA5 charges rows are computed on Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's £3,002, meaning every monetary figure in the Standard Life row of the charges table is wrong. This is a high-severity finding: the largest cost justification for switching Standard Life is built on a miscalculated base and contradicted by the report's own narrative. Second: no safeguarded benefits analysis is present for any ceding plan. The extraction confirms these paths are NotPresentInDocument. While [P9] (Zurich) confirms no transfer charge and [P13] confirms no MVR, there is no documentation in the file confirming absence of guaranteed annuity rates, protected pension ages, protected tax-free cash, or other safeguarded benefits for Zurich, Aviva, Standard Life, or People's Pension. The Scottish Widows response ([P14]) mentions protected tax-free cash (confirmed absent for Scottish Widows), but equivalent confirmation is absent for the other plans being transferred. This is a high-severity omission per the decision logic. Third: Standard Life's payment history ([P16]) shows employer contributions, indicating a former workplace scheme. The report does not address whether this scheme is still active with employer contributions, or whether any employer matching would be lost. If the scheme is dormant this matters less, but the report is silent. Fourth: the immediate death benefit shortfall of £3,305 post-switch ([P7]) is documented in the switch report but is not addressed in the suitability report, which is a material omission. Fifth: the mid-growth rate discrepancy (2.94% vs 2.95%) is minor and unlikely to be material but is present. The Zurich and combined-plan comparisons in [P3] and [P6] do provide per-plan projections, and the overall structure of the switch report does include alternative comparisons (retain Aviva only, retain Standard Life only, retain Zurich only), which partially addresses the alternatives consideration. The benefits of switching — lifestyling risk, fund alignment at risk level 5, consolidation — are plausible and corroborated by the policy research [P13]. However, the combination of: (a) a self-contradictory and arithmetically wrong Standard Life cost comparison, (b) no safeguarded benefits analysis for any transferring plan, and (c) no discussion of the immediate death benefit shortfall, means the evidence does not fully support the switching justification. The vulnerability overlay: no specific vulnerability indicators have been identified in the evidence that would further complicate the switching recommendation, though the client's stated desire to simplify his arrangements ([P13]) is a relevant client-specific factor that supports consolidation.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension: £127,000","New solution: £125,000","Critical yield: 3.2%","Effect of switching: £2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension: £127,000","New solution: £115,000","Critical yield: 4.8%","Effect of switching: £12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","